{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1551736-should-garou-characters-have-influence-as-a-background","id":1551736,"title":"Should Garou characters have Influence as a Background?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-06-17T08:33:44-07:00","id":1551736,"title":"Should Garou characters have Influence as a Background?","content":"As the title says.<br /> <br /> I was just reading the Backgrounds of other splats, to see if some of them could be used in W20, and it got me thinking? Should the Garou have Influence as a Background?<br /> <br /> V20 treats this just as political influence, but M20 expands the Background to other areas, too. Media, criminal underworld, and so on. <br /> <br /> Now, I'm aware most Garou are not close enough to human society to exert influence over mortals, but thare are some important exceptions: Glass Walkers, CoG, Silver Fangs, and Shadow Lords.<br /> <br /> So, what do you think? Should Garou from those tribes have the Influence Background? Yes? No? Why?<br /> <br /> Or perhaps should it be restricted to kinfolk PCs and NPCs? <br /> <br /> Just curious to know what everyone here thinks. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","name":"Greywolf76","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28735_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-17T11:09:27-07:00","id":1551738,"title":"","content":"I'm not the biggest fan of the Influence Background in the cWoD so... that colors my perceptions of this.<br /> <br /> I think, ultimately, a huge amount of this comes down to how easy you want to make Garou-human interactions. Some people like the game where the Garou can hold down jobs, be part of human society, and so on; even with effort. In that style of WtA, Influence makes more sense. Some people like the Garou - even Tribes that put more energy into it - struggling with being direct parts of human society. So Influence makes less sense. There isn't much &quot;struggling with your Rage&quot; if you can plop a few freebies into Influence.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-17T12:03:19-07:00","id":1551740,"title":"","content":"The Influence background in tabletop was always nebulous. It was a a general placeholder for any kind of mortal influence where the vampire who had it could get things done. It was meant to be purposefully vague so it could be tailored to any individual PC or NPC need. When LARP brought in a large number of players in one game, it quickly became apparent that the vague generic Influence background was leading to conflict and arguments, so the LARP rules quickly broke it down into various categories and this has been replicated in a lot of online games (and occasionally tabletop). That clarified things in games that had large numbers of people. The specific breakdown of Influence can be useful, but also unfairly restricting. In small tabletop games, the gaming group should use whatever method works best for them.<br /> <br /> There are two kinds of influence. One is &quot;direct&quot; influence which reflects the actual power and influence someone has publicly. You can control a corporation because you own it or are the CEO. You are also head of the local industry lobbying group so you have some sway with your fellow business leaders. You donate to politicians and host fundraisers so you have some clout with local politicians and friendships with similar people. Humans will almost always have Influence of this nature. Whatever it is they have, it is because if what they are doing themselves and sway with the people they know. <br /> <br /> &quot;Influence&quot; in this case (as a background) is probably best left to only the most powerful and influential human NPCs. Most people who have a more restricted/reasonable &quot;power&quot; is probably best left undefined as a mechanic (a clerk at the DMV can do certain things, but it's best to not provide them a background to mechanically explain it). And characters who know such someone is best defined with the Allies background.<br /> <br /> The other kind of influence is indirect. It is a hidden or secret influence. This is usually where vampires come into play because they have so many supernatural powers at their disposal, particularly Dominate, Presence, and the blood bond. This influence can be deep and wide because your pawns (who have direct influence) can be many and varied, and only activated once you need them. This is usually the case with the Influence background - the character has lots of secret pawns and can act as the string puller.<br /> <br /> Garou typically have a problem with influence because of the Curse (which is often ignored in practice by many STs and players). It is hard to form bonds with humans if they are always afraid of being murdered by you. However, I can also see some limited areas where such a reputation is not a detriment and may even be useful (low levels in Influence within a criminal organization for example). There are some Gifts that can help, but most Gifts give advantage in tactical situations, not long term secret pawns. However, in most cases such &quot;influence&quot; is probably best simulated with Allies and Contacts, not Influence.<br /> <br /> In general, I believe the Kinfolk background is meant to be the closest thing to &quot;Influence&quot; for most Garou. Kinfolk can provide a ridiculous number of individual kinfolk helping you, and most players will blanch at providing character names and brief description for the 50 kinfolk you can get at Kinfolk 5. Some combination of Kinfolk, Allies, and Contacts (the last two can be justified as being kinfolk as well) will almost always provide most of what a Garou needs to do in a game session. <br /> <br /> Also remember that in a lot of cases, no single Garou will &quot;control&quot; a single entity aligned with the tribe or sept. Very often another (or more!) Garou in that same tribe or sept will have their own Kinfolk, Allies, and Contacts in that same organization or social sphere. There can be lots of people drawing water from the same well. In a small town of kinfolk, the sept effectively controls all the local politics, commerce, churches, and so on. That will provide complete domination of the local area in a way that NO vampire with high Influence could ever do. The mechanical backgrounds are not the setting. They are merely tools to provide ways for individual characters to utilize the setting.<br /> <br /> That is what I would use in the vast majority of cases of Garou even in cases where the local Shadow Lord controls one of the major local organized crime factions, a Glass Walker who owns a powerful local business, or a Children of Gaia who operates a network of environmental protest groups. I don't think you actually need Influence to reflect the kind of favors a Garou can extract. Nor do I think the selection of Background depends on what tribe is used. There's no problem for any tribe to have one member that controls local politics because one of their Kinfolk is mayor of the town, or they have kinfolk on the tribal reservation council, or anything else. I've never liked the tribal restrictions found in the early editions of the game which said this tribe can't have this or that background. I thought it was mostly arbitrary. You can justify a lot with any single character concept even if it mostly out of place for the tribe as a whole.<br /> <br /> Does this mean the Influence background never makes sense? Sometimes it can, and if the ST thinks it is best to utilize that background go ahead. However, in most cases I think utilizing some combination of Kinfolk, Allies, and Contacts probably works best for most Garou.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-17T12:29:40-07:00","id":1551742,"title":"","content":"Basically what Heavy Arms said. By itself the Background isn't a big problem for Garou. If it works for your game, go for it.<br /> <br /> Yet it isn't the most thematic Background for a werewolf. Indirect Influence through Allies, Retainers or Contacts may be more interesting, as it reinforces the need to use Kinfolk as intermediaries, even for (still Rage fueled) urban or diplomatic Garou.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-18T05:54:46-07:00","id":1551752,"title":"","content":"Thanks, everyone, for the responses.<br /> <br /> I agree that many GMs / Storytellers just shrug off the Curse, and that's unfortunate. Even in my campaigns, where I completely disregard avoid the most grimdark and nihilist aspects of the game, the Curse is present, and I make it very clear to my players that a rage 5+ ahroun will have a very hard time interacting with mere mortals (at lest while he is in the lower ranks), and most Ragabash and Philodox are better suited to act as the pack's &quot;face&quot; while interacting with humans.<br /> <br /> There is also a terrible side effect of a GM / ST ignoring the Curse: if PCs can freely interact with the human society, the players tend to disregard kinfolk as unimportant, which is a shame. In my own experience running WW games, kinfolk are not only extremely important to Garou society, as a whole, but essential to PCs. Most high Rage, low Rank Garou just need their human kin to interact humans on a daily basis (I also have a house rule that, while in the presence of their kinfolk, a Garou's curse is not as strong). <br /> <br /> Anyway, that's why I asked about Influence - a few ago I was thinking about the Curse and how it affects PCs / NPCs interactions, and that might be why Influence is not usually available to werewolves as a Background. It makes sense.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, I really liked how Laws of the Wild treated Influence, and although I've never LARP'ed, I think I might adapt that to kinfolk NPCs (even if it will serve just to give me, as a ST, some gauge of how much clout said kinfolk might have).<br /> <br /> Once again, thanks a lot for your responses. Really appreciated. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","name":"Greywolf76","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28735_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-29T13:19:02-07:00","id":1552728,"title":"","content":"I know I'm kinda necro-posting and I apologize, but honestly, I think it would be more appropriate to assign the Influence background to a Kinfolk, then have that kinfolk be how your character gets a dot in resources (or some other equivalent). Not saying don't, but hear me out:<br /> <br /> Basically... Use human kinfolk.<br /> <br /> (I know someone else already said this, but) Do cool stuff with them to help bridge the gaps between your Garou players and Humanity. If a player character has a family business that they're trying to keep afloat but are competing against the local O'Tolly's, then there still needs to be somebody managing the cash register and being your players' Alibi if they do decide to take action against a Pentex shell company and not get singled out.<br /> <br /> Also, Kinfolk in 20th Anniversary are hilariously broken in the best way because according to &quot;Kinfolk: A Breed Apart&quot;, they can, IN THEORY, learn every single rank 1 gift in the game. Spirit Speech is also Rank 1 and every spirit can teach it.<br /> <br /> I love Kinfolk and they're highly underrated.<br /> <br /> But yes, it seems everyone else has already said what I would've otherwise pointed out, but I felt compelled to throw my own two cents in here anyway.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28838,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28838-dafrontlinespecter","user":"DaFrontlineSpecter","date":"?) 07-29-2025, 04:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28838-dafrontlinespecter","name":"DaFrontlineSpecter","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-29T13:47:25-07:00","id":1552730,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure it's fair to say the Kinfolk are underrated by anyone other than very narrow-minded Garou; there's a difference between underrated and being subjected to an overly protective paternalistic society that forces you into a secondary position for your own safety. It is, however, worth noting that the Kinfolk books are generally meant for exceptional kinfolk (aka PCs) and aren't representative of the vast majority of kinfolk.<br /> <br /> Even then the &quot;can learn Rank 1 Gifts&quot; thing is not as good as it sounds (it is good, there are some great Rank 1 Gifts like Persuasion that are never not worth getting if you can) because anything that requires Gnosis is even more restricted (and likely not nearly as useful due to small ratings in the trait), and anything that involves Rage is right out. If you're making a Kinfolk that has magically healing abilities, learning one of the healing Numina is a far better option than dumping everything into max Kin Gnosis and Mother's Touch.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-29T18:11:01-07:00","id":1552740,"title":"","content":"I did heard once or twice of players that have the same paternalistic myopic view of Kinfolk as the more narrow-minded characters, but that's wild and quite rare. They're rightly well regarded by players in general, and their capacity to make stuff happen is very important.<br /> <br /> The thought of using Influence by the Garou directly probably comes more from the fact that in RPGs in general players tend to try to be as self-sufficient as possible. Having the ability to do stuff in your own character sheet isn't always the best rationale, but is the most intuitive.<br /> <br /> Other than that, yeah, Kinfolk have a lot of options. And they're likely to face less supernatural opposition to their powers, so they're very effective in practice. Looking out for Numina is a general good idea, though, instead of hunting down Gifts all the time.<br /> <br /> Anything that uses Willpower instead of Gnosis will give them more bang for their buck, by the way.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-31T11:05:41-07:00","id":1552778,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1551736-should-garou-characters-have-influence-as-a-background?p=1552728#post1552728\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I know I'm kinda necro-posting and I apologize,</div> </div> </div> </div>No need to apologize. Thanks for sharing your thoughts.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1551736-should-garou-characters-have-influence-as-a-background?p=1552728#post1552728\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">but honestly, I think it would be more appropriate to assign the Influence background to a Kinfolk, then have that kinfolk be how your character gets a dot in resources (or some other equivalent).</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. As I said before, I think that is the best approach. It makes Kinfolk more necessary and valued than ever.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1551736-should-garou-characters-have-influence-as-a-background?p=1552730#post1552730\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure it's fair to say the Kinfolk are underrated by anyone other than very narrow-minded Garou; there's a difference between underrated and being subjected to an overly protective paternalistic society that forces you into a secondary position for your own safety.</div> </div> </div> </div>Funny you mentioned that, Heavy Arms. And I've got what you're saying, but while we're at it, I know that's pretty much the &quot;default&quot; in canon, but I never liked (and, as a storyteller, never used) this &quot;Kinfolk as cattle&quot; or &quot;Kinfolk as second-rate people&quot;. My games tend to be less &quot;grimdark&quot; and more heroic in tone, so my Garou (at least most of them) treat their Kinfolk as valued and loved family members. Messing with a Garou's kin is the same thing as painting a target on your forehead. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Also, because of the Curse, most werewolves need their Kin as liaisons and go-betweens just to deal with human society on a daily basis. <br /> <br /> Players who neglect Kinfolk as an important part of Garou society tend to be in a huge disadvantage when dealing with the mortal world.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","name":"Greywolf76","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28735_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-31T13:02:44-07:00","id":1552781,"title":"","content":"I mean, no matter how egalitarian minded a Garou is, the fact of their existence does create a hierarchy where the kinfolk can't compete. Kinfolk can't personally empower rites, can't hold most Sept positions, can't have Rank, etc. The most skilled Kinfolk - even with a few Gifts - is still going to struggle taking on a relatively focused BSD in the same purview.<br /> <br /> But that's sort of how the &quot;loving and valued family members,&quot; isn't incompatible with a paternalistic attitude among the Garou. The Garou want to protect their loved ones from enemies that their loved ones can't protect themselves from. It's not grimdark... and even built into any sort of heroic narrative... that the &quot;good guys&quot; are necessary to stop the &quot;bad guys,&quot; for the better of everyone else.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-31T19:25:23-07:00","id":1552790,"title":"","content":"The main point in the end is that Garou and Kinfolk are superlative at their jobs in comparison to the other. But the Garou job is war, and the military tend to become paternalistic with civilians.<br /> <br /> Some Garou treat kinfolk as cattle or slaves, but that isn't necessarily (or even likely) the default, certainly most Garou think of them as family. But that doesn't mean they won't be paternalistic. They see their loved ones as being in grave danger, from beings they have no hope of surviving an encounter with, that plays a number with your head.<br /> <br /> Of course, the more open-minded Garou may be able to see the problems with thinking too much like that. They may recognize not only the role of Kinfolk, but their ability to at least evaluate the risks and make their own decisions. But sticking to it against the anxiety of watching your loved ones assume risks isn't so simple for everyone. Especially when the trauma of the horrors they see regularly start to build up.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-01T06:37:31-07:00","id":1552796,"title":"","content":"I had just written a huge post commenting <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> and <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> posts above, but it somehow disappeared before I had the chance to click the &quot;Post Reply&quot; button.<br /> <br /> Oh, well. The gist of it was...<br /> <br /> That I pretty much agree with you both said.<br /> <br /> That I really liked the &quot;military x civilian&quot; analogy.<br /> <br /> That it's completely understandable that a given Garou would be overzealous, overprotective, and, yes, even paternalistic towards his Kinfolk, because - let's face it - even Kinfolk gifted with Numina or Gifts, or highly skilled and trained, would be no match for most of the things a werewolf fights on a daily basis. A single fomori? Maybe. A vampire or BSD? No way. Besides, having Kinfolk fighting by their side would be more of a distraction than anything, not to mention it could give their enemies an easy target.<br /> <br /> And that Garou can be overzealous and overprotective without being paternalistic or dismissive. After all, most Kinfolk just want to play their part in the fight against the Wyrm. The key is finding them a proper battle to fight - and that doesn't make going to war in a literal sense.<br /> <br /> Also, I wanted to add how much I liked the functions or archetypes provided in the <i>Kinfolk - A Bree Apart</i> W20 book (you know, The Teacher, The Sentinel, etc.). I've done something similar in my campaigns before, but it's great to see it in print, and it's a prefect example of how Kinfolk can be helpful and useful to Garou without putting themselves in the line of fire. <br /> <br /> And, while I'm at it, I'd also add another archetype to that list, <b>The Influencer</b>, a Kinfolk who has a lot of human influence on human society (represented by the Influence Background, if you want to fully develop him or her as a NPC). As I said above, the richly detailed version of the Influence Background from MET would be perfect for this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28735,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","user":"Greywolf76","date":". 08-01-2025, 09:49 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","name":"Greywolf76","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28735_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-01T12:36:43-07:00","id":1552810,"title":"","content":"I feel like there's a bit of a disconnect in language here. I'm not sure most people would consider there to be a meaningful distinction between the Garou being overly protective of their kinfolk, and the Garou being paternalistic towards them. While they are not 100% identical and interchangeable concepts, what usually makes paternalism unique from going overboard with otherwise positive emotional desires towards another is social power dynamics. When the Garou and kinfolk existing in a society where the Garou are granted inherent superior social standing, it creates a situation where it doesn't really feel like there's much of a difference in that context.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-03T09:00:15-07:00","id":1552855,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1551736-should-garou-characters-have-influence-as-a-background?p=1552810#post1552810\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I feel like there's a bit of a disconnect in language here. I'm not sure most people would consider there to be a meaningful distinction between the Garou being overly protective of their kinfolk, and the Garou being paternalistic towards them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not exactly. As I understand, although both paternalism and over-protectiveness involve interfering with someone's autonomy, they are quite different in both motivation and scope.<br /> <br /> Paternalism is a broader concept, and involves interference based on a belief that this is done for the person's best interest.<br /> <br /> Over-protectiveness, on the other hand, is more specific, often driven by fear or anxiety, and primarily focused on preventing harm (or potential harm) to another person. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","name":"Greywolf76","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28735_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-03T13:29:12-07:00","id":1552859,"title":"","content":"OK.... so first off, it feels kinda weird to quote my post and then cut out the part that actually addresses what you're saying: that whatever the differences you want to make between paternalism and over-protectiveness on a semantic level, you're not addressing the context of the setting.<br /> <br /> Beyond semantics, I think there's a more fundamental epistemological issue with this. Labeling someone as either (or any similar assessment) is essentially indirect knowledge. You can't directly observe &quot;protectiveness&quot; or quantify it to measure when it exceeds norms. These are assessments inherently based on judgements of context and behavior. No matter how well you can define these concepts to be as clear as possible as which applies... it's which applies in a specific context.<br /> <br /> Is a Garou telling a kin that they need to stay in the Sept Bawn because it's too dangerous right now for them to be away from protectors paternalistic? Over-protective? Just being an asshole because they can and they don't really care? Completely reasonable? You can't say objectively without mind-reading powers, and you can't make a well reasonable determination subjectively without context.<br /> <br /> Even if we go inside our own minds. You can't do something you think is inappropriate. Your internal narrative to translate motivations into actions has to include some rationale for why something you might normally consider unacceptable is at least in the moment of that context. You can accept other people might not agree. You can self reflect and later conclude you were mistaken in your thinking. But if you truly thought something was wrong to do you wouldn't do it. So even internally, we don't make objective judgements of our actions as we do them. We think we're doing something within the bounds of our self-perceptions, and maybe we're wrong and maybe we're right.<br /> <br /> We can take these even into our fictional characters were we can have them do things for bad reasons consciously. Just because an author intends for a character to be &quot;paternalistic&quot; doesn't mean that's how things will end up. As the story goes on, intentions and plot might conflict and intention gives way to a different mode of behavior. The audience might just not see the actions the way the author intended. TTRPGs in particular can render such intentions trivial. If I want to make a particularly paternalistic Garou as a character flaw, but the ST only puts us in situations where the kin need protection despite their protests... that character flaw never really comes up.<br /> <br /> Putting a lot of weight into what's over-protectiveness and what's paternalism just doesn't hold up to the nature of the conversation and our ability to ever fully assess which term is most correct.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-03T23:18:20-07:00","id":1552872,"title":"","content":"At the end of the day, the problem is the power dynamics.<br /> <br /> Garou are more powerful, physically, mystically and socially. They're the special beings, they experience things Kinfolk don't have the means to, they hold both the authority, the means to enforce it and monopoly on the information needed to justify it.<br /> <br /> So whatever the reason behind a Garou's actions and disposition, whatever their intent, unless they deliberately give up authority, and do so in a meaningful, recognizable, effective and real way, it still boils down to the Garou saying what goes and the kinfolk obeying without recourse. And this power dynamics have long term consequences regardless of the intentions and rationale behind it.<br /> <br /> For a Garou to really change this is something far harder than simply caring about the kinfolk's opinion. It takes a lot of conscious and careful effort to change the power dynamics, to actively empower the kinfolk in the relationship and give up your own autonomy in order to share it. And it can still be done wrong, so it also takes a lot of effort into re-evaluating and correcting course during the process.<br /> <br /> This isn't impossible, by any means. But it isn't a simple thing, either. And that's what the majority of the Garou aren't prepared to do, as even beyond disposition it takes a lot of preparation and a particular world-view and philosophy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-20T07:26:41-07:00","id":1553287,"title":"","content":"I think the best (or most cursed) comparison I've seen was when someone else explained it to me like this:<br /> <br /> Garou are basically to oWoD, what Space Marines are to 40k<br /> (EXTREME EXAGGERATION, but there's parallels)<br /> There's plenty of crazy and horrifying stuff that can 1-shot you (and I don't mean the angry guys with the shotguns)<br /> <br /> And if the Garou are the &quot;Space Marines&quot;<br /> Then the Kinfolk are Chapter Serfs (but cooler and with a few more perks)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28838,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28838-dafrontlinespecter","user":"DaFrontlineSpecter","date":"comment. lol 08-20-2025, 10:36 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28838-dafrontlinespecter","name":"DaFrontlineSpecter","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-20T12:14:35-07:00","id":1553297,"title":"","content":"I tend to go toward BattleTech's Clans for comparisons with sci-fi miniatures games. Both have warrior cultures that are expressly not up to dealing with everything that they need to and live to bask in glory that they mostly get through combat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-20T23:24:46-07:00","id":1553318,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1551736-should-garou-characters-have-influence-as-a-background?p=1553287#post1553287\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the best (or most cursed) comparison I've seen was when someone else explained it to me like this:<br /> <br /> Garou are basically to oWoD, what Space Marines are to 40k<br /> (EXTREME EXAGGERATION, but there's parallels)<br /> There's plenty of crazy and horrifying stuff that can 1-shot you (and I don't mean the angry guys with the shotguns)<br /> <br /> And if the Garou are the &quot;Space Marines&quot;<br /> Then the Kinfolk are Chapter Serfs (but cooler and with a few more perks)</div> </div> </div> </div>ehre is noh time to beh lohst!<br /> <br /> Batul Brothas!<br /> <br /> Garoues, todeh the enemeh is at oua doar! We know oua duteh and we will do eet. We fight for our honor as Savor Fanges,<br /> as GAROUES, and we fight in the nehme of Ghaya!<br /> <br /> And if we die this deh we die in gloareh, we die heroes' deffs, but we shall not die, no! It is the enemeh who will tehste deff and defeat! As you know''<br /> <br /> Moast of oua battle brothars are shtehtioned in UMBRAA, Pruhpeared to deep strike! Oua perimeter has been<br /> pruhpeared in the even dat oua enehmies should be so bald and so foolish. We have plehced numerous merroirs , allowing for<br /> muhltiple, simuln-tehneous and devashtehting defensive deep strikes ouhr ansestors namees this maneuvah Merroir Fhang. We will descend upon the foe, we will ovawhelm them - we will leave none<br /> alive! Meanwhile oua ground fawses will ensue the full defense of oua headkwaters<br /> <br /> We are the Garoues! WE ARE THE GHAYA'S FHANGES!<br /> <br /> -Except from the script of relic's latest RTS project: Werewolf the Apocalypse: <a href=\"https://youtu.be/LJMLfACod48?si=Yxv9AHTdwvU-sKQC\" target=\"_blank\">Dawn of Rage</a><br /> <br /> But taking things somewhat back on topic I actually picture them being closer to the orkz and eldar. Fight happyness for the former and the whole dying race (and mistreatment by they authors) of the latter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude02","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25848-mrornx","name":"MrornX","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"418"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}